INT. CONVERTED CINEMA FIELD HOSPITAL - NIGHT

A former movie palace. The velvet seats have been ripped out and replaced with rows of cots. The screen at the front is a mural of water-stained gold curtains. Plastic sheeting hangs from the balcony. Generator hum. Coughing.

DR. PRIYA NAIR (40s, scrubs two sizes large, hair under a surgical cap) crosses the aisle, clipboard pressed to her chest. She is the only one moving fast.

A NURSE, SEBASTIAN OYELOWO (30s, soft-spoken, glasses taped at the hinge), trails her with a tray.

At the center of the stage, a boy lies on a cot, wrapped in a foil blanket. The boy, ten, flushed and unconscious, does not stir.

Beside him, a MAN in a stained suit sits slumped on a folding chair. THEO ABBASI (50s, the boy's father, unshaven, wedding ring turning round his finger) grips the cot's rail.

She reaches the cot. Checks the chart. He watches her read.

PRIYA
How long has the fever been over a hundred and three?

THEO
Since the lights went out.

SEBASTIAN
Four hours.

She lays a hand on the boy's forehead. He winces. She tilts his head, thumbs back an eyelid. He doesn't resist.

PRIYA
(quietly, to Sebastian)
Start the line. Saline, then the second antibiotic.

SEBASTIAN
We're out of the second.

She does not turn. Her shoulders stiffen. She breathes in once.

PRIYA
Then the first, double. And find me a pharmacist.

SEBASTIAN
There are no pharmacists.

PRIYA
Then find me someone who used to be one.

He nods and leaves. The generator stutters. Lights dim, brighten. Theo looks up.

THEO
Doctor.

PRIYA
Mr. Abbasi.

THEO
Is he going to make it?

She looks at him. Not at the chart, not at the boy. At him.

PRIYA
I don't know yet.

He nods. It is the answer he expected, but he needed to hear it said. He lowers his head onto the rail.

Priya straightens. Across the hall, a second cot. A second father. A nurse waving frantically.

She walks toward it. Then stops. Turns back. Kneels beside the boy and tucks the foil blanket tighter, careful of his arm.

PRIYA (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Stay with us, little one.

He does not stir.

She stands. The generator coughs again. This time the lights go out entirely.

Darkness. Someone sobs. A match flares. Another. A third. Sebastian reappears, holding a candle, a woman beside him in a grey cardigan, shaking out her hands.

SEBASTIAN
Doctor, this is Ms. Halloran. She ran a pharmacy for thirty years.

Priya takes in the cardigan, the shaking hands, the tired eyes. She puts out her own.

PRIYA
Do you remember how to mix a pediatric dose?

The woman almost smiles.

MS. HALLORAN
Like my own name.

PRIYA
Good. Because tonight, you're the only name I trust.

The woman nods, rolls up her sleeves, and follows Sebastian into the dark. Priya stands over the cot. The candle flames lean.

THEO
(not looking up)
Thank you.

PRIYA
Don't. Not yet.

She stays beside the boy, one hand on the rail, her eyes on the rise and fall of the foil. The mural curtains hang above them, golden, ruined, waiting for a show that never begins.
